{"cluster_id": "C-3764", "topic": "Consolidated `ca-fsck` exits nonzero for `dangling`, `chain_broken`, `corrupte", "target": "operations/debugging", "member_ids": ["B003-066", "B034-093"], "canonical_claim": "`ca-fsck` exits nonzero for `dangling`, `chain_broken`, `corrupted_runs`, or `lifeless_keys`, but never exits nonzero solely for `stale_edge`; a zero exit code therefore does not prove a clean pool without checking `stale_edge`. Additionally, `ca-fsck` was deliberately kept non-fatal for `stale_edge` findings pending a separate retention-leak decision.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/08-testing-and-soak.md#ca-fsck", "docs/superpowers/cas/BACKLOG.md#q3-stale-edge-nonfatal"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-3765", "topic": "Consolidated `ca-gc-dryrun` requires read-only mode, calls `previewDeletes()` ", "target": "operations/debugging", "member_ids": ["B002-072", "B003-068", "B004-077"], "canonical_claim": "`ca-gc-dryrun` requires read-only mode, calls `previewDeletes()` with fixed `Gc` ID `UInt128(1)`, and performs no writes. Additionally, `clickhouse-disks ca-gc-dryrun --disk <ca_disk_name>` is read-only and derives next-round delete candidates from `gc/snap` and `gc/state` without CAS writes or deletes. Additionally, `ca-gc-dryrun` is read-only and write-free, can over-report away from quiescence because it does not fold new owner events, and its output must never drive a real delete.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/08-testing-and-soak.md#gc-dryrun", "docs/superpowers/cas/11-walkthrough.md#gc-observability", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/04-ops-mounts.md#e-ca-inspect-ca-gc-dryrun"]}
